WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:08.564
If you think in SQL, you write SELECT first, and the database picks the order it really runs in.

2
00:00:08.733 --> 00:00:14.224
DQL, the Dynatrace Query Language, reads top to bottom, in the order it runs.

3
00:00:14.224 --> 00:00:16.149
That is the biggest change.

4
00:00:16.933 --> 00:00:20.472
Grail is the store behind everything in Dynatrace.

5
00:00:20.472 --> 00:00:27.336
It keeps logs, spans, events, problems and metrics side by side, and you read all of it with DQL.

6
00:00:27.533 --> 00:00:30.853
A DQL query is a pipeline, like a shell pipeline.

7
00:00:30.853 --> 00:00:33.224
The first command produces records.

8
00:00:33.224 --> 00:00:38.711
Each pipe hands them to the next command, which filters, reshapes or counts them.

9
00:00:39.467 --> 00:00:41.401
Start with the data command.

10
00:00:41.401 --> 00:00:44.232
It builds a small table inside the query.

11
00:00:44.232 --> 00:00:46.857
Six records, with path, status and ms.

12
00:00:46.857 --> 00:00:50.587
Grail scans nothing, so everyone gets the same result.

13
00:00:51.133 --> 00:00:52.738
Add a filter on a new line.

14
00:00:52.738 --> 00:00:57.611
Records flow down the pipe, and filter keeps the ones where the condition is true.

15
00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:01.222
Only two records have a status of 500 or more.

16
00:01:01.222 --> 00:01:02.784
The others stop here.

17
00:01:02.933 --> 00:01:04.559
Note the operators.

18
00:01:04.559 --> 00:01:06.441
Equality in DQL is ==.

19
00:01:06.441 --> 00:01:11.060
A single = gives a field a name, as you will see next.

20
00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:14.197
Now replace the filter with summarize.

21
00:01:14.197 --> 00:01:18.107
It groups the records by path, and computes one row per group.

22
00:01:18.267 --> 00:01:20.390
Four records share the path /.

23
00:01:20.390 --> 00:01:22.867
One each have /healthz and /readyz.

24
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:30.516
The four records of / fold into one row: 4 requests, 1 error, and an average of 100 milliseconds.

25
00:01:30.667 --> 00:01:33.595
Each result gets a name with a single =.

26
00:01:33.595 --> 00:01:35.864
And the by list sits in braces.

27
00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:38.073
Add a computed field.

28
00:01:38.073 --> 00:01:44.605
fieldsAdd keeps every column, and adds one: errors times 100, divided by requests.

29
00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:47.433
Then sort by the name you gave it.

30
00:01:47.433 --> 00:01:53.240
The worst path comes first: /readyz at 100 percent, / at 25, /healthz at 0.

31
00:01:54.933 --> 00:01:55.919
Order matters.

32
00:01:55.919 --> 00:01:59.648
A command only sees what the one before it passed on.

33
00:01:59.648 --> 00:02:03.167
After summarize, only the summarized fields exist.

34
00:02:03.333 --> 00:02:04.493
So filter first.

35
00:02:04.493 --> 00:02:08.190
And watch out: several SQL habits do not even fail.

36
00:02:08.190 --> 00:02:12.975
They quietly return zero rows, and zero rows looks like no errors.

37
00:02:13.667 --> 00:02:17.480
Now put fetch logs, from minus one hour, where data was.

38
00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:19.931
The same commands work on real logs.

39
00:02:20.067 --> 00:02:21.770
Only the first line changed.

40
00:02:21.770 --> 00:02:25.541
The commands below it do not care where the records came from.

41
00:02:25.733 --> 00:02:28.469
Grail charges queries by the data they scan.

42
00:02:28.469 --> 00:02:31.080
And the scan happens in the first command.

43
00:02:31.267 --> 00:02:33.522
One hour of logs is a small scan.

44
00:02:33.522 --> 00:02:38.853
from minus thirty days, over all logs, costs real money on a paid environment.

45
00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:43.965
So a narrow time window at the start matters more than anything later in the pipe.

46
00:02:43.965 --> 00:02:46.992
Use the shortest window that answers the question.

47
00:02:47.733 --> 00:02:49.315
Read DQL top to bottom.

48
00:02:49.315 --> 00:02:56.536
The first command fetches, each pipe passes the records on, and a short time window keeps the scan small.
